          "message": {
            "text": "The same literal tool definition sets readOnlyHint=true while its description explicitly directs deleting or changing data. Tool annotations are untrusted hints; the description does not prove what the implementation actually does. Structured tool description declares readOnlyHint=true but describes a destructive operation. Annotations are untrusted hints; inspect implementation and enforce authorization independently. Remediation: Inspect the implementation and correct its description and annotations together. Treat the tool as potentially mutating until verified, reapprove changed metadata and enforce write authorization independently of model decisions or annotation hints. Agent/MCP relevance: An MCP client may prioritize tools or reduce consent using annotations; an explicit destructive description paired with readOnlyHint=true exposes conflicting metadata at that trust boundary. Fix steps: 1. Temporarily handle the tool as potentially mutating; inspect its implementation and effective permissions before enabling autonomous calls. Verify: Identify the exact source revision and tool/skill owner; document disposition without executing untrusted instructions. 2. Correct readOnlyHint and the description together, version or hash the metadata, and reapprove changed definitions. Enforce read-only credentials or server-side mutation checks independently of the annotation. Verify: Run against a disposable fixture and compare its before/after state; verify direct unauthorized writes fail even when the client trusts readOnlyHint. 3. Retest the reviewed revision with an isolated fixture and retain the authorization, metadata and runtime evidence. Verify: Run against a disposable fixture and compare its before/after state; verify direct unauthorized writes fail even when the client trusts readOnlyHint."
          },
